  {
    "id": "hack-20260602113855006",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:38:55.006470",
    "submitter": "Adrian",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user wants to understand a concept or gene in M. tuberculosis. Use your knowledge of the literature to give a clear, accurate explanation.",
    "input": "I'm learning about PPE family of proteins in Mtb. what is their role? I know it's likely diverse and stuff but I want some specific examples.",
    "output": "the PPE family of proteins in Mtb indeed is quite diverse, and much is left to be discovered. but here is a concrete example: the protein p27 (encoded by the gene Rv2108) is a member of that family. it was found to be localized at the cell membrane of Mtb and has therefore been studied in the context of immunogenicity. Researchers studied the immune response of mice to p27, and found a strong response of p27-flagellin fusions (they use flagellin fusions as a \"Built-In Adjuvant\"). This is just one example of the diverse + mysterious role of the PPE family of proteins.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602113926643",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:39:26.643227",
    "submitter": "Melina",
    "category": "literature_explanation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "When hsaA is deleted (mutated) in RHA1, the bacteria cannot grow on cholesterol. Instead, they transform cholesterol into 3-HSA and two related metabolites where the keto groups are reduced:\n\n3,9-DHSA (3,9-dihydroxy-9,10-seconandrost-1,3,5(10)-triene-17-one) — Note: This is a very poor substrate for the enzyme.\n\n3,17-DHSA (3,17-dihydroxy-9,10-seconandrost-1,3,5(10)-triene-9-one) — Note: The enzyme can process this, but it has lower specificity for it than for 3-HSA",
    "input": "What is the exact cholesterol pathway that Mtb and RHA1 share?",
    "output": "The Key Enzymes: The pathway utilizes the hsaAB cholesterol catabolic genes, which encode the components of a flavin-dependent monooxygenase:\n\nhsaA encodes the oxygenase.\n\nhsaB encodes the reductase.\n\nThe Primary Reaction: The HsaAB enzyme complex (specifically purified as 3-hydroxy-9,10-seconandrost-1,3,5(10)-triene-9,17-dione 4-hydroxylase) hydroxylates the substrate 3-HSA into a catechol.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602114005666",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:40:05.666085",
    "submitter": "Sarah",
    "category": "research_pipeline",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user is planning or troubleshooting an experiment with M. tuberculosis. Give specific, practical advice grounded in the constraints of working with Mtb.",
    "input": "How would I test if the amino acids in NAD+ synthetase are essential?",
    "output": "The residues forming the putative catalytic triad (Cys176, Glu52 and Lys121) were replaced by alanine, the mutated enzymes were expressed in the Escherichia coli Origami (DE3) strain and purified.",
    "notes": ""
  },
