  {
    "id": "hack-20260602114525132",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:45:25.132866",
    "submitter": "Nate",
    "category": "hypothesis_generation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given an experimental observation in M. tuberculosis, propose the most plausible molecular mechanisms and suggest an experiment that would distinguish between them.",
    "input": "Given that RsfA activation is triggered by a reducing environment, under what specific physiological conditions or host intracellular situations would we expect RsfA to be activated in M. tuberculosis?",
    "output": "This cascade is likely triggered when M. tuberculosis encounters heavily reducing microenvironments, such as the severely hypoxic, necrotic core of a mature host granuloma. Alternatively, this reducing state may occur as a metabolic \"all-clear\" signal inside host macrophages after the bacterium has successfully neutralized the host's initial oxidative burst using its internal antioxidant defenses.\n\nTo confirm these hypotheses, researchers could generate an M. tuberculosis mutant strain expressing a non-reducible variant of RsfA by mutating the key cysteine residues (e.g., $\\text{Cys73Ala}$ or $\\text{Cys109Ala}$), preventing the formation and subsequent reduction of the crucial disulfide bond. This mutant strain, alongside a wild-type control, would be subjected to both anaerobic, hypoxic chamber conditions (mimicking the granuloma) and post-oxidative stress challenges inside activated macrophage cell cultures. By utilizing Western blotting or mass spectrometry to track UsfX–RsfA complex formation, paired with RT-qPCR to measure the expression of downstream SigF-regulated genes, researchers can verify if blocking RsfA reduction successfully halts SigF activation and decreases bacterial survival during hypoxia and post-stress recovery.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602114610928",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:46:10.928229",
    "submitter": "Ricardo",
    "category": "research_pipeline",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user has a question about experimental design or protocol for TB research. Answer with the specificity of someone who has worked under BSL-3 conditions.",
    "input": "Protein–protein interactions control the diverse and essential molecular processes inside the cell. To maintain the cellular physiology, protein kinases not only signal their substrates through reversible phosphorylation, but they also physically interact with them. PknI, a serine/threonine protein kinase of Mycobacterium tuberculosis is known to be important for cellular homoeostasis. In this study, we have identified the interacting proteins for PknI. We screened for proteins interacting with PknI using an in vitro assay, Far-western blot. This protein kinase specifically interacts with two peroxidase proteins of M. tuberculosis, Rv2159c and Rv0148. The PknI–Rv2159c interaction pair was further studied for the critical amino acid residues in Rv2159c that are responsible for the interaction. Rv2159c, a hypothetical protein is predicted to be an antioxidant with peroxidase activity. We performed homology modelling of Rv2159c protein and molecular docking using multiple docking servers such as Z-Dock and ClusPro. Further, the most favorable conformation of PknI–Rv2159c interaction was obtained using molecular dynamics simulation. The critical amino acid residues of the Rv2159c involved in interaction with PknI were identified. Mutation and docking analysis showed that the Ala1–Gly2–Trp3 residues in Rv2159c structure are responsible for the interaction. The free binding energy between the wild type and mutant complexes using MM-GBSA has provided insight about the stability of PknI–Rv2159c interaction. We propose that, PknI physically interacts with Rv2159c both in vitro and in silico studies.",
    "output": "We screened for proteins interacting with PknI using an in vitro assay, Far-western blot. This protein kinase specifically interacts with two peroxidase proteins of M. tuberculosis, Rv2159c and Rv0148. The PknI–Rv2159c interaction pair was further studied for the critical amino acid residues in Rv2159c that are responsible for the interaction. Rv2159c, a hypothetical protein is predicted to be an antioxidant with peroxidase activity. We performed homology modelling of Rv2159c protein and molecular docking using multiple docking servers such as Z-Dock and ClusPro. Further, the most favorable conformation of PknI–Rv2159c interaction was obtained using molecular dynamics simulation. The critical amino acid residues of the Rv2159c involved in interaction with PknI were identified. Mutation and docking analysis showed that the Ala1–Gly2–Trp3 residues in Rv2159c structure are responsible for the interaction.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602115006145",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:50:06.145791",
    "submitter": "Sarah",
    "category": "closed_book_qa",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user wants to understand a concept or gene in M. tuberculosis. Use your knowledge of the literature to give a clear, accurate explanation.",
    "input": "How is 2,3-diacyltrehaloses (DAT) formed?",
    "output": "The formation of DAT occurs on the cytosolic face of the plasma membrane through the action of PapA3, FadD21, and Pks3/4",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602115032809",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:50:32.809744",
    "submitter": "adriana",
    "category": "research_pipeline",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user has a question about experimental design or protocol for TB research. Answer with the specificity of someone who has worked under BSL-3 conditions.",
    "input": "Why did they use estriol for the bond structure?",
    "output": "CYP51 is a sterol 14 alpha- demethylase, estriol lacks a methyl group in the 14 position. This is a good substrate analog to mimic the native sterol binding.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602115059397",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:50:59.397084",
    "submitter": "Melina",
    "category": "research_pipeline",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Expression and purification of recombinant His-tagged proteins. Over-expression of the cloned genes was induced following incubation of BL21 strain carrying the expression plasmids with 1 mM IPTG for 4 h at 37 °C. The recombinant proteins were produced in the soluble form in Escherichia coli BL21 and purified on Ni-NTA columns (Qiagen) as recommended by the supplier. The final elutions were checked by SDS–PAGE for purity before dialyzing with 20 mM Tris–HCl at pH 7.2 containing 1 mM DTT and 5% glycerol.",
    "input": "What were the conditions for the in vitro activity assay of PknH kinase phosphorylation?",
    "output": "In vitro kinase assay. The in vitro kinase reactions contained 25 mM Tris–HCl, pH 7.2, 5 mM MgCl2, 2 mM MnCl2, 1 mM DTT, 10–300 ng of recombinant PknH kinase, and 0–500 nM of substrates. The reactions were started by addition of 10 μCi of γ-[32P]ATP (Perkin-Elmer) followed by 5–30 min incubation at room temperature (21 °C). At the end of the incubation period, reactions were stopped by the addition of SDS-sample loading buffer and heated at 95 °C for 5 min. Samples were resolved by SDS–PAGE on 8% polyacrylamide gels and the gels were silver stained, and dried. The 32P-radioactively labeled protein bands were detected using a PhosphorImager SI (Molecular Dynamics). Bands corresponding to the phosphorylated proteins were cut out and subjected to scintillation count (Beckman Coulter LS 6500).",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602115112800",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:51:12.800965",
    "submitter": "Sarah",
    "category": "closed_book_qa",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user wants to understand a concept or gene in M. tuberculosis. Use your knowledge of the literature to give a clear, accurate explanation.",
    "input": "How is penta-acyltrehaloses (PAT) formed?",
    "output": "The formation of PAT occurs on the periplasmic face via transesterification reactions between DAT substrates catalyzed by the acyltransferase Chp2 (Rv1184c).",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602115333453",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:53:33.453314",
    "submitter": "Sarah",
    "category": "closed_book_qa",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user wants to understand a concept or gene in M. tuberculosis. Use your knowledge of the literature to give a clear, accurate explanation.",
    "input": "What happens when mmpL10 or chp2 activity is disrupted in mtb?",
    "output": "Disruption of mmpL10 or chp2 leads to an important build-up of DAT inside the cells and to the formation of a novel form of unsulfated acyltrehalose esterified with polymethyl-branched fatty acids normally found in sulfolipids that is translocated to the cell surface.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602115641073",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:56:41.073605",
    "submitter": "Ricardo",
    "category": "closed_book_qa",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user has a question about a TB paper or finding. Correct any misconceptions and guide them toward the right way to think about it.",
    "input": "What causes the changes of stoichiometry in heme degradation of MhuD ?",
    "output": "Structural comparison between MhuD–diheme and inactive IsdG and IsdI bound to only one highly distorted metalloporphyrin ring reveals that several residues located in α-helix 2 and the subsequent loop appear to be responsible for heme stoichiometric differences",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602115933904",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T11:59:33.904482",
    "submitter": "Nate",
    "category": "gene_target_prioritization",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user is inquiring about developing a drug to target a specific pathway. Help them prioritize which protein in this pathway to target and develop an experiment to test drugs targeting this protein.",
    "input": "We are looking to develop a novel small-molecule therapeutic that disrupts the M. tuberculosis environmental stress-response pathway to prevent virulence and latency. Which specific gene or protein domain in this pathway should we prioritize as a drug target, and what is the biological rationale for this choice?",
    "output": "Rv1364c should be prioritized as the primary drug target because it represents a highly specialized, single-chain multi-tool that consolidates three separate regulatory functions—phosphatase, kinase, and anti-anti-sigma factor—into a single protein. Targeting Rv1364c offers a distinct therapeutic advantage over multi-protein partner-switching systems found in other bacteria; disrupting the delicate internal tug-of-war between its kinase and phosphatase domains will collapse the entire environmental stress-response cascade at once. Specifically, designing a small molecule that binds the interdomain communication interface or locks the kinase domain in a constitutively active state would artificially suppress the alternative sigma factor, rendering M. tuberculosis unable to adapt to host microenvironments and preventing the transition into chronic dormancy.\n\nTo validate Rv1364c as a viable drug target, researchers can generate a conditional knockdown strain of M. tuberculosis using a tetracycline-inducible CRISPR interference (CRISPRi) system to precisely control the depletion of the Rv1364c gene. This knockdown strain would be evaluated alongside a wild-type control in an in vitro model of infection—such as exposing the bacteria to low pH, reactive oxygen species, and hypoxia inside activated human macrophages. By tracking bacterial survival via colony-forming unit (CFU) counts and monitoring the expression of downstream alternative sigma factor genes via RT-qPCR upon Rv1364c depletion, researchers can confirm whether silencing this single-chain regulator significantly compromises bacterial fitness and successfully halts the stress-adaptation response during host infection.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602120323650",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T12:03:23.650271",
    "submitter": "Sarah",
    "category": "closed_book_qa",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user wants to understand a concept or gene in M. tuberculosis. Use your knowledge of the literature to give a clear, accurate explanation.",
    "input": "Why does thiolactomycin negatively impact Mycobacterium tuberculosis (mtb) survival?",
    "output": "TLM targets two β-ketoacyl-acyl-carrier protein synthases, KasA and KasB, consistent with the fact that both enzymes belong to the fatty-acid synthase type II system involved in fatty acid and mycolic acid biosynthesis.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602120606368",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T12:06:06.368642",
    "submitter": "Melina",
    "category": "result_interpretation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "They confirmed the active-site cysteine was in its reduced, reactive form using DTNB (Ellman's reagent), which titrates free thiol groups. This was a key functional check since the catalytic Cys is essential for condensation activity and is sensitive to oxidation.",
    "input": "How did they confirm the purified proteins were in their native conformational state rather than partially denatured?",
    "output": "Both KasA and KasB were expressed as His-tagged fusions in E. coli and purified by Ni-affinity chromatography. Native state was inferred primarily from enzymatic activity — the proteins were catalytically competent, which itself implies correct folding of the active site.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602120741457",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T12:07:41.457082",
    "submitter": "adriana",
    "category": "closed_book_qa",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a passage from the TB literature and a researcher's request, explain the key findings in clear, accessible terms without oversimplifying the biology.",
    "input": "Is azole commonly used to target CYP51, why are azoles becoming less effective?",
    "output": "Azoles are antifungal drugs that inhibit CYP51. Azole resistance occurs when mutations in CYP51 or related pathways prevent the drugs from working effectively.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "hack-20260602120959646",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-02T12:09:59.646441",
    "submitter": "Nate",
    "category": "result_interpretation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user has shared experimental results from a TB study. Interpret the biological significance of their hits, flag likely artifacts, and suggest what to follow up.",
    "input": "We are purifying variants of the M. tuberculosis single-chain fusion protein Rv1364c from E. coli. When we run our isolated kinase-substrate domain fragment ($\\Delta\\text{PAS}\\Delta\\text{PP2C}$) on a gel and stain it with Pro-Q Diamond phosphoprotein stain, we see a massive fluorescent signal. However, when we run our full-length wild-type protein, the phosphorylation signal drops nearly to baseline. Puzzlingly, when we introduce a single point mutation into the phosphatase domain ($\\text{D328A}$), the full-length protein suddenly retains a massive, intense phosphorylation signal. What biochemical phenomenon explains why the $\\text{D328A}$ mutation causes the protein to accumulate phosphate groups?",
    "output": "Your data demonstrates a dynamic, intramolecular kinetic antagonism between the protein's own kinase and phosphatase domains. The isolated kinase domain fragment readily phosphorylates its attached substrate domain during expression in E. coli. In the full-length wild-type protein, this signal disappears because the functional environmental phosphatase domain immediately removes those phosphate groups as fast as the kinase can add them. By introducing the $\\text{D328A}$ mutation, you successfully inactivated the phosphatase domain's catalytic core, breaking this internal loop and trapping the protein in its autophosphorylated state.\n\nTo confirm that this autophosphorylation is strictly an intramolecular (cis) reaction occurring within the same chain rather than an intermolecular (trans) reaction between neighboring Rv1364c dimers, researchers can perform a mixed-in vitro kinase assay. They would purify a kinase-dead/phosphatase-dead double mutant (e.g., $\\text{E444K/D328A}$) which has a valid phosphorylation site but no active domains, and incubate it with an active fragment containing only the kinase domain. If the standalone kinase domain fails to transfer radiolabeled ATP ($\\text{[}\\gamma\\text{-}^{32}\\text{P]ATP}$) to the independent dead mutant in trans, it confirms that the native phosphorylation loop requires the kinase and substrate modules to be physically tethered on the same single chain.",
    "notes": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-hyp-001",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:36:03.059483",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "hypothesis_generation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given an experimental observation in M. tuberculosis research, propose the most plausible molecular mechanisms that could explain it, and suggest a specific experiment that would distinguish between them.",
    "input": "I'm studying ESAT-6 and CFP-10, the two major secreted antigens that are co-expressed and form a tight 1:1 heterodimer in M. tuberculosis. When I test each protein in isolation against macrophages and type 2 pneumocytes, ESAT-6 alone is strongly cytolytic, but CFP-10 alone shows no detectable cytolytic activity — even though in vivo they're always found together. What's going on? Why would one half of a constitutive heterodimer be lytic and the other completely not?",
    "output": "The asymmetry makes sense if ESAT-6 is the membrane-active subunit and CFP-10's primary role is elsewhere — likely as a secretion chaperone or a regulator of when and where lytic activity is unleashed.\n\nTwo mechanisms are worth thinking through. The first is that ESAT-6 contains an intrinsic membrane-disrupting element — probably an amphipathic helix — that directly intercalates into the host cell bilayer, and CFP-10 simply lacks this structural feature. On this model, what you're seeing in vitro is the default activity of the isolated lytic domain, and the heterodimer might actually be the attenuated or delivery-competent form. The second possibility is that the complex acts as a regulated system: ESAT-6 is kept latent while bound to CFP-10 and is only activated upon dissociation — triggered, for instance, by the acidic pH of the phagosome or by contact with a specific membrane composition. CFP-10 would then be more of a safety catch than an equal partner in lysis.\n\nTo distinguish these, I'd run two experiments. First, map the minimal lytic domain on ESAT-6 by systematic truncation and alanine scanning of candidate amphipathic helices, then confirm the identified region is necessary and sufficient for membrane disruption in a liposome leakage assay. Second, engineer a disulfide-locked ESAT-6·CFP-10 complex that cannot dissociate, and compare its cytolytic activity to free ESAT-6 across a pH gradient mimicking the phagosomal environment. If the locked complex is non-lytic at neutral pH but regains lytic activity at pH 4.5–5.5, that strongly supports a pH-triggered dissociation model.",
    "notes": "Grounded in ESAT-6/CFP-10 characterization literature. Cytolytic asymmetry is documented. Verify amphipathic helix mapping references and confirm CFP-10 is truly non-lytic in isolation before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-hyp-002",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:36:03.059493",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "hypothesis_generation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given an unexpected structural or functional observation in M. tuberculosis, propose the most plausible molecular explanations and suggest an experiment to test the leading hypothesis.",
    "input": "I've been characterizing the two GroEL homologues in M. tuberculosis — Cpn60.1 and Cpn60.2. Unlike canonical E. coli GroEL which assembles into a 14-mer, the Mtb homologues appear to exist predominantly as lower oligomers — dimers, tetramers — yet they still suppress protein aggregation. This is really unusual for a chaperonin. The canonical double-ring architecture is supposed to be essential for the encapsulation mechanism. Why would Mtb evolve GroEL homologues that break this rule and still function?",
    "output": "The deviation from the 14-mer is surprising because the double-ring architecture is generally considered essential for ATP-driven substrate encapsulation. But there are a few explanations worth considering.\n\nOne is that the Mtb Cpn60 homologues operate by a different mechanism altogether — holdase rather than foldase activity. Holdase chaperones suppress aggregation by binding exposed hydrophobic patches on unfolded proteins without enclosing them, and this doesn't require the full tetradecameric barrel. If Mtb Cpn60 acts primarily as a holdase, the oligomeric requirement is relaxed and the lower assemblies make sense. A second possibility is environmental adaptation: the 14-mer may be disfavored at the low oxygen tensions and temperatures Mtb encounters inside the granuloma, and the smaller assemblies may be better suited to that specific physicochemical environment. A third angle is functional specialization between the two paralogs — Mtb encodes both Cpn60.1 and Cpn60.2, and it's possible that one acts as the canonical foldase while the other is the aggregation suppressor, so neither alone needs the full architecture.\n\nI'd start by testing the ATP-dependence of aggregation suppression. Canonical foldase activity requires ATP hydrolysis; holdase activity does not. If Mtb Cpn60 suppresses aggregation equally well in the presence of ATPγS, that strongly supports the holdase model. As a follow-up, use analytical ultracentrifugation under granuloma-mimicking conditions — low oxygen, acidic pH, 33°C — to map whether oligomeric state shifts under physiological Mtb conditions relative to standard in vitro conditions.",
    "notes": "Mtb GroEL lower oligomers documented (Qamra et al. 2004). Two paralogs groEL1/groEL2 established. Verify whether ATPase activity is retained in lower oligomers and confirm which paralogs were studied before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-hyp-003",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:36:03.059496",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "hypothesis_generation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given an observation about differential regulation in M. tuberculosis, propose the most plausible molecular mechanisms and suggest a specific experiment to test the leading hypothesis.",
    "input": "I'm working on iron acquisition in Mtb. We're seeing something interesting: when heme-binding/uptake proteins like FecB2 are depleted, siderophore-based iron acquisition genes get upregulated — and the reverse seems true too. The two pathways look inversely regulated depending on which iron source is available. What mechanism could explain this cross-regulation, and how would you test it?",
    "output": "Inverse regulation between two competing iron acquisition routes makes good biological sense — Mtb needs iron constantly, so sensing which source is available and upregulating the appropriate pathway is an efficient solution.\n\nThe most parsimonious explanation is a shared iron-responsive regulator that senses overall cellular iron status rather than pathway-specific signals. IdeR is the canonical candidate: it represses iron acquisition genes under iron-replete conditions and derepresses them under starvation. If heme and siderophore acquisition genes are both under IdeR control but with different promoter affinities or operator architectures, a shift in the available iron source could differentially tune their expression without requiring any direct pathway cross-talk. A second possibility is that successful heme import generates a metabolic signal — free porphyrin or heme-derived iron — that directly inhibits siderophore biosynthesis enzymes or their transcriptional activators, creating substrate-level feedback rather than a central regulator.\n\nTo test the IdeR model, I'd perform ChIP-seq with an IdeR antibody under heme-replete and siderophore-replete conditions separately, comparing occupancy at promoters of FecB2 and key siderophore biosynthesis genes (e.g., mbtB, mbtI). If IdeR occupancy shifts in a way that mirrors your expression data, that supports the shared-regulator model. As a clean genetic test, an IdeR constitutive-derepression mutant should abolish the differential regulation — both pathways should be maximally expressed regardless of iron source, flattening the inverse relationship you're observing.",
    "notes": "FecB2 as heme-uptake pathway component is documented. IdeR as iron-responsive regulator in Mtb is well established. mbt operon for siderophore biosynthesis is IdeR-regulated. Verify whether FecB2 promoter contains an IdeR operator before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-hyp-004",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:36:03.059499",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "hypothesis_generation",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given an unexpected finding about an M. tuberculosis enzyme, propose the most plausible mechanistic explanation and suggest how you would further characterize it.",
    "input": "We've been characterizing Rv2131c and found something surprising — it has both inositol monophosphatase and fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase activity in the same active site. These are usually treated as distinct enzymes with different substrates. Both activities depend on the same catalytic aspartate (D94), and mutating it kills both. How does one active site accommodate two structurally different substrates, and why would Mtb maintain a bifunctional enzyme here rather than two specialized ones?",
    "output": "Dual-specificity phosphatases that share a catalytic mechanism but accept different substrates are unusual but not unprecedented. What usually explains them is that the active site is optimized for the chemical transformation — phosphate ester hydrolysis — rather than for tight substrate discrimination. Both inositol-1-phosphate and fructose-1,6-bisphosphate are phosphorylated carbohydrates, so if the active site primarily engages the phosphate group and a small flanking region, promiscuous activity toward both sugar backbones is structurally plausible. The conservation of D94 as the catalytic residue for both reactions is consistent with this: the aspartate likely acts as the nucleophile in both cases via a shared mechanism.\n\nThe more interesting question is why Mtb encodes a bifunctional enzyme rather than two specialized ones. One possibility is metabolic economy — Mtb has a compact genome and a bifunctional enzyme has a biosynthetic advantage. A more biologically compelling possibility is coordinated regulation: inositol phosphate metabolism feeds into phosphatidylinositol mannoside biosynthesis (a key cell envelope component), while FBPase activity sits at a gluconeogenic node. Having both under the control of a single enzyme means a single regulatory input could simultaneously tune both pathways — useful when Mtb switches carbon sources during intracellular growth.\n\nTo probe this, I'd try to engineer separation-of-function mutants — active-site substitutions that impair one activity without the other. If such mutants can be made, expressing them in an Rv2131c deletion strain and testing growth in macrophages versus axenic culture would directly address which activity is load-bearing under infection conditions and whether the bifunctionality is genuinely required or just tolerated.",
    "notes": "Rv2131c dual IMPase/FBPase activity is documented. D94 as catalytic residue confirmed by mutagenesis. Connection to phosphatidylinositol mannoside biosynthesis is inferred — verify before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-001",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151192",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I work on thiol redox biology — thioredoxins, glutaredoxins, that sort of thing. I'm looking to branch into a disease-relevant system. Is there anything particularly interesting about how Mtb handles oxidative stress that would be relevant to my work?",
    "output": "Mtb has an unusually interesting thiol redox system. Unlike most bacteria that rely on glutathione, Mtb uses mycothiol as its primary low-molecular-weight thiol — a unique metabolite that's biosynthetically and structurally distinct. There's a whole mycothiol-dependent redox network built around it: mycothiol S-transferases, mycoredoxins (the functional analogue of glutaredoxins), and the mycothiol disulfide reductase Mtr. The system parallels what you know from glutaredoxin biology but with distinct chemistry, and several of the enzymes are essential with no close human homologue — which makes them attractive targets. I'd start with the literature on mycothiol biosynthesis and the Mtr/mycoredoxin pathway; the mechanistic parallels to the glutaredoxin system should make it straightforward to map onto your existing framework.",
    "notes": "Mycothiol as primary LMW thiol in Mtb is well established. Mtr and mycoredoxin pathway documented. Verify essentiality claims for specific enzymes before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-002",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151203",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I study bacterial secretion systems — specifically Type III. I'm trying to expand into mycobacteria. What should I know about protein secretion in Mtb? Is there an equivalent?",
    "output": "Mtb doesn't have a Type III secretion system, but it has something arguably more interesting — the ESX or Type VII secretion systems, of which it encodes five paralogs (ESX-1 through ESX-5). ESX-1 is the most studied and the most important for virulence: it secretes ESAT-6 and CFP-10, which are involved in phagosomal membrane disruption and are among the dominant T cell antigens. ESX-3 is essential and required for iron and zinc acquisition. ESX-5 handles secretion of a large fraction of the PE/PPE protein family. The architecture is completely different from Type III — there's no needle complex, and substrates use a WXG100 motif for recognition. The structural biology of the ESX complexes has moved fast recently; I'd start with the cryo-EM structures of ESX-3 and ESX-4, which give the clearest picture of the secretion mechanism.",
    "notes": "Five ESX paralogs in Mtb well established. ESX-1/ESAT-6 virulence role and ESX-3 essentiality documented. Verify which ESX structures are most recently solved before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-003",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151207",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I'm a microbiologist who works on bacterial persistence and antibiotic tolerance. I want to understand how Mtb specifically handles non-replicating persistence — I know it's a major clinical problem but I don't know the molecular details well.",
    "output": "Mtb's non-replicating persistence is one of the most clinically significant problems in the field — it's the main reason TB treatment takes 6 months. The Wayne model is the standard in vitro system: progressive hypoxia drives Mtb into a non-replicating state with a specific transcriptional response called the DosR (or DevR) regulon. DosR is a response regulator activated by nitric oxide and CO as well as oxygen limitation, and it upregulates around 50 genes thought to maintain viability under dormancy. Metabolically, the bacterium shifts dramatically — accumulating lipid stores, downregulating biosynthetic pathways, and upregulating energy-conserving pathways. The reason dormant Mtb is so hard to kill is that most antibiotics target active processes (cell wall synthesis, DNA replication, transcription) that are largely shut down. I'd point you to the DosR regulon literature and the Wayne hypoxic persistence model as the foundation, then look at the newer work on the distinction between antibiotic tolerance and classical persistence in Mtb.",
    "notes": "DosR/DevR regulon and Wayne model are well established. Activation by NO/CO in addition to hypoxia documented. Verify current consensus on ~50 DosR target genes before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-004",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151210",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I'm an organic chemist who works on polyketide natural products. I know mycobacteria make unusual lipids but I don't know much about the enzymology. What's interesting about Mtb's polyketide machinery from a chemistry standpoint?",
    "output": "Mtb has one of the most elaborate lipid biosynthetic machineries of any known bacterium — genuinely interesting territory for a polyketide chemist. A few things stand out. First, Mtb unusually encodes both a giant type I FAS (FAS-I, a single multifunctional polypeptide that makes C16-C26 fatty acids) and a dissociable type II FAS system (FAS-II), which extends FAS-I products to make the very-long-chain meromycolyl chains that go into mycolic acids. Most bacteria have one or the other, not both. Second, there's a family of modular type I PKS enzymes — Pks1, Pks2, Pks12, and others — that assemble unusual virulence-associated lipids including phthiocerol dimycocerosates (PDIMs) and sulfolipids. The PpsA-E assembly line for PDIM is one of the most thoroughly biochemically characterized PKS systems in any pathogen. I'd start with the biochemistry of the Pps cluster and the mycolic acid biosynthesis literature — the FAS-I/FAS-II handoff is particularly well documented.",
    "notes": "FAS-I/FAS-II co-occurrence in Mtb well established. Pks/Pps cluster for PDIM is documented. PDIMs as virulence-associated lipids confirmed. Verify specific Pks gene assignments before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-005",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151212",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I work on AAA+ proteases — ClpXP specifically. I'm looking for a new bacterial system to expand into. Is ClpP important in Mtb, and is there anything unusual about protein degradation in mycobacteria?",
    "output": "Mtb's protease biology is genuinely unusual in a couple of ways you'd find interesting. First, Mtb encodes two ClpP paralogs — ClpP1 and ClpP2 — and both are required for activity. They form a mixed tetradecameric ClpP1P2 complex, which is atypical; most bacteria have a single ClpP that forms a homo-oligomeric barrel. The hetero-oligomeric architecture has been studied structurally and the two subunits have different but complementary active-site chemistry. Second, and more unusually, Mtb has a functional proteasome — it's one of the very few bacteria known to have one, and it's essential for pathogenesis. Substrates are tagged via pupylation, a post-translational modification that's functionally analogous to ubiquitination: the small protein Pup is ligated to substrate lysines by PafA. The Pup-proteasome system is important for Mtb's resistance to nitric oxide stress inside macrophages. If you work on ClpXP, the mixed ClpP1P2 complex is a natural entry point — the structural literature on it is well developed.",
    "notes": "ClpP1P2 hetero-oligomeric complex in Mtb documented. Proteasome and pupylation system established. Essentiality of ClpP1P2 for Mtb viability confirmed. Verify structural details of mixed barrel before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-006",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151214",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I'm a structural biologist looking for new drug targets to work on in TB. I've been working on DNA gyrase inhibitors — is gyrase a good TB target, or would my expertise be better applied somewhere else in Mtb?",
    "output": "Gyrase is actually one of the better-validated targets in Mtb — fluoroquinolones target the GyrA subunit and are used in second-line TB treatment, so there's clinical proof of concept. The Mtb gyrase has structural differences from E. coli gyrase that affect inhibitor selectivity, and the structural literature is well developed. That said, fluoroquinolone resistance is a defining feature of XDR-TB and is growing, so novel binding sites are the real opportunity — the GyrB ATPase domain is less exploited than the GyrA quinolone resistance-determining region. If you want adjacent targets where structural work is still early: Mtb topoisomerase I (TopA, Rv3646c) has no close human homologue and has attracted interest, and Mtb's DNA repair machinery has some unusual gaps — it lacks several canonical repair enzymes found in E. coli — which has raised interest in its recombination machinery. I'd look at the structural comparison of Mtb vs. E. coli GyrB as a starting point, and the emerging literature on Mtb TopA.",
    "notes": "Fluoroquinolones targeting GyrA in Mtb and their role in XDR-TB is well established. GyrB as alternative site and TopA as target are in the literature. Verify Rv3646c as TopA gene assignment before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-007",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151216",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I study the stringent response — (p)ppGpp signaling. Mostly in E. coli and B. subtilis. Is the stringent response relevant in Mtb and is there anything unusual about it compared to what I already know?",
    "output": "The stringent response is very relevant in Mtb and has a couple of distinctive features. Mtb encodes two (p)ppGpp synthetases: RelMtb (a bifunctional synthetase/hydrolase, structurally similar to E. coli RelA), and a smaller alarmone synthetase RelZ. RelMtb is important for long-term survival under nutrient starvation and has been linked to the non-replicating persistence phenotype — rel mutants fail to properly enter dormancy and are cleared faster under starvation conditions than wild type. One thing that's notable is that RelMtb appears to be activated not just by ribosome stalling on uncharged tRNAs (the canonical mechanism) but also by fatty acid limitation, which makes sense given how central lipid metabolism is to Mtb. The structural biology of RelMtb is well developed — synthetase and hydrolase domains have been characterized separately. I'd start with the RelMtb literature on starvation survival and the comparison to the canonical E. coli stringent response.",
    "notes": "RelMtb bifunctional synthetase/hydrolase and RelZ small synthetase documented. Rel mutant persistence defect established. Fatty acid starvation activation needs verification against primary literature before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-008",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151218",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I'm a lipid biochemist. I've heard Mtb can catabolize cholesterol from the host — is that real, and how well characterized is the pathway enzymatically?",
    "output": "It's real, and the cholesterol catabolism pathway in Mtb is one of the most thoroughly worked-out biosynthetic pathways in the organism. Mtb can use cholesterol as a sole carbon and energy source, and the pathway is encoded largely by a genomic island associated with intracellular growth. The side chain is removed first through a beta-oxidation-like process, then the steroid rings are opened sequentially by a series of oxygenases and hydrolases. Several enzymes are now structurally characterized — HsaC (a Rieske-type oxygenase), HsaD (the meta-cleavage carbon-carbon hydrolase), and the KshA/KshB two-component steroid hydroxylase are among the best studied. The clinical relevance is that cholesterol catabolism appears important for Mtb survival inside macrophages, where cholesterol is abundant, and several pathway enzymes are essential for intracellular growth and have been proposed as drug targets. The Mce4 transporter is thought to mediate cholesterol import. I'd start with the biochemical literature on HsaC, HsaD, and KshA/B, and the studies linking the igr locus to intracellular survival.",
    "notes": "Cholesterol catabolism pathway well established in Mtb. HsaC, HsaD, KshA/B structurally characterized. Mce4 as cholesterol importer proposed. Verify essentiality claims for specific enzymes during intracellular growth before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-009",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151221",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I do Tn-seq and CRISPRi screens to find essential genes in bacteria. I'm just starting to work with Mtb. What do I need to know that's different from E. coli or B. subtilis — any surprises in how essentiality works in Mtb?",
    "output": "A few things will surprise you coming from E. coli. First, Mtb has a large set of conditionally essential genes — genes that are dispensable in axenic culture but required inside macrophages. The original TraSH and later Tn-seq screens identified a substantial number of genes needed for in vivo growth that were missed entirely in broth screens, so your screen design needs to match the condition you care about. Second, the cell envelope dominates the essential gene landscape in a way you won't see in E. coli — mycolic acid biosynthesis, arabinogalactan synthesis, and peptidoglycan remodeling are heavily represented, reflecting how central and unique the mycobacterial cell wall is. Third, the slow growth (24-hour doubling time) means depletion screens need long time courses — at least 10–15 generations to see clear signal for essential genes. There are also GC-richness effects on transposon insertion bias to account for. I'd look at the Griffin et al. 2011 and DeJesus et al. 2017 essential gene datasets as baseline references before designing your screen.",
    "notes": "TraSH/Tn-seq conditional essentiality in Mtb documented. Cell envelope gene enrichment in essential gene sets established. Griffin 2011 and DeJesus 2017 are key Tn-seq references. Verify doubling time and generation count recommendations before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-010",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151223",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I'm a cell biologist studying selective autophagy. I want to move into an infection context. How does Mtb interact with autophagy — does it just get degraded, or is there more going on?",
    "output": "Mtb actively subverts autophagy, which makes it a genuinely interesting system for your work. When a macrophage takes up Mtb, the bacterium arrests phagosomal maturation — it blocks fusion with lysosomes and prevents compartment acidification, so it never reaches the degradative environment. ESX-1 is involved in this: ESAT-6 permeabilizes the phagosomal membrane, which triggers cytosolic surveillance and can activate xenophagy. But Mtb has counter-measures — there's evidence it can tolerate or escape the autophagosome. On the host side, the standard autophagy machinery (Beclin-1, LC3, ULK1) is involved, but there are Mtb-specific intersections: p62/SQSTM1 and NDP52 have been implicated as cargo receptors, and vitamin D signaling has been shown to boost autophagy-mediated Mtb killing. The interplay between ESX-1-mediated membrane damage and the subsequent autophagy response is probably the most mechanistically interesting entry point. I'd start with the literature on Mtb phagosomal arrest and ESX-1-dependent cytosolic access, then look at the studies on LC3-associated phagocytosis as a distinct pathway from canonical xenophagy.",
    "notes": "Mtb phagosomal maturation arrest well established. ESX-1/ESAT-6 phagosomal permeabilization documented. p62 and NDP52 as cargo receptors in Mtb xenophagy in literature. Vitamin D/autophagy connection documented. Verify LC3-associated phagocytosis vs xenophagy distinction before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-011",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151225",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I study biofilm formation — matrix composition, regulatory circuits, the works. Is biofilm relevant in Mtb? I've seen something about a pellicle but I don't know how developed the field is.",
    "output": "Mtb biofilm is a real and active area of research, though less mature than the E. coli or P. aeruginosa fields. In vitro, Mtb forms a pellicle at the air-liquid interface in standing culture that is transcriptionally and structurally distinct from planktonic growth. The most unusual aspect from a matrix chemistry standpoint is that the Mtb biofilm matrix appears to be largely composed of free mycolic acids and other lipids rather than polysaccharides — which is very different from what you're used to and reflects the lipid-dominated cell envelope generally. Transcriptomically, biofilm growth involves upregulation of lipid metabolism genes and overlaps with the dormancy transcriptome, which has led to interest in whether pellicle/biofilm growth contributes to antibiotic tolerance in vivo. There's also emerging evidence for biofilm-like structures inside granulomas, though that's harder to study. On the regulatory side, WhiB4 and the iniB locus have come up. I'd look at the biofilm transcriptomics literature and the work characterizing the mycolic acid-based matrix composition — that's where the most distinctive Mtb-specific biology is.",
    "notes": "Mtb pellicle biofilm formation documented. Lipid-based matrix composition proposed. Overlap with dormancy transcriptome in literature. WhiB4 and iniB regulatory connections need verification against primary literature before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "gen-rc-012",
    "submitted_at": "2026-06-04T14:49:17.151227",
    "submitter": "claude",
    "category": "research_consult",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a researcher's description of their background, question, or goal, respond helpfully and substantively using your knowledge of M. tuberculosis.",
    "input": "I work on CD8 T cell responses to intracellular pathogens. I want to understand the TB side better — specifically how Mtb interacts with MHC class I antigen presentation, and what's known about the antigens recognized by CD8 T cells.",
    "output": "Mtb engages CD8 T cells through MHC class I, which requires antigen to reach the cytosol — and ESX-1 is central to this. ESAT-6 permeabilizes the phagosomal membrane, allowing Mtb proteins to leak into the cytosol where they can be proteasomally processed and loaded onto MHC class I. CD8 T cells specific for ESAT-6, Ag85B, and other Mtb antigens have been characterized in infected individuals and in mouse models. A complication is that Mtb appears to actively suppress MHC class I presentation in infected macrophages — there's evidence that infection reduces surface MHC class I levels, possibly via interference with TAP-dependent peptide loading. On the antigen side, the PE/PPE protein family is thought to contain targets for both CD4 and CD8 responses, but antigenic variation across strains makes them difficult to study systematically. The distinction between cross-presentation (by uninfected DCs taking up Mtb antigens) and direct presentation (by infected macrophages) is also important in Mtb — cross-presentation is thought to be a major pathway for CD8 priming. I'd start with the ESX-1-dependent cytosolic access literature and the studies on Mtb-specific CD8 T cell responses in humans.",
    "notes": "ESX-1/ESAT-6 phagosomal permeabilization enabling MHC-I presentation is documented. Mtb suppression of MHC-I in the literature. Cross-presentation as major CD8 priming route in Mtb proposed. Verify TAP interference claim specifically before bootstrapping."
  },
  {
    "id": "claude-methcrit-001",
    "submitted_at": "2026-08-06T00:00:00",
    "submitter": "Claude",
    "category": "methodology_critique",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user describes a planned or completed experimental approach involving M. tuberculosis. Critically evaluate the methodology: identify missing controls, confounds, or design flaws, and suggest concrete improvements.",
    "input": "Hey, I'm testing whether a candidate virulence gene affects Mtb survival inside macrophages. My plan: make a knockout of the gene, infect THP-1 macrophages with the KO and wild-type strains side by side, and measure bacterial CFU at 24h and 72h post-infection. Does this design hold up?",
    "output": "It's a reasonable starting design but a few gaps would make reviewers (and you) doubt the result. Biggest one: you need a complementation strain — reintroduce the gene at its native locus or on a plasmid in the KO background. Without it, you can't rule out polar effects on downstream genes in the same operon, or a stray second-site mutation picked up during knockout construction, being the real cause of any phenotype. Second, check and report your MOI at time 0 for both strains — a KO can have altered surface properties that change phagocytic uptake, which would confound a survival readout that's really an uptake difference. Third, standardize THP-1 differentiation (PMA concentration and duration) tightly, since differentiation state changes macrophage killing capacity. I'd also ask whether CFU alone tells you what you want — it conflates replication and killing, so a fluorescent reporter strain or paired CFU/RNA readout can help separate the two. Finally, THP-1 is an immortalized line with known quirks; if the phenotype is real, it's worth confirming in primary human monocyte-derived macrophages or in vivo before leaning on it too hard.",
    "notes": "AI-drafted seed (Claude) — needs Adrian/domain review before use in a large-scale generation run."
  },
  {
    "id": "claude-methcrit-002",
    "submitted_at": "2026-08-06T00:00:00",
    "submitter": "Claude",
    "category": "methodology_critique",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user is planning a drug susceptibility or growth-inhibition experiment with M. tuberculosis. Review their protocol for methodological gaps and suggest fixes.",
    "input": "I want to test a new compound's activity against Mtb. Plan: grow Mtb in 7H9 broth, add the compound at a single concentration, and check OD600 after a week compared to untreated. If OD is lower, I'll call it active.",
    "output": "A few things I'd fix before running this. A single concentration only tells you active-or-not at that dose, not potency — run a serial dilution and get an MIC (MIC90 is the usual TB convention) so the result is comparable to the literature. OD600 is also a weak readout for Mtb specifically: it grows slowly and clumps, so optical density is a noisy proxy for viable count. CFU plating or a resazurin-based assay like MABA/REMA is more standard and more sensitive. You're also missing a vehicle control (whatever solvent you dissolved the compound in, usually DMSO) to rule out solvent toxicity, and a positive control (rifampicin or isoniazid at a known-active concentration) to confirm the assay itself is behaving that day. I'd test against H37Rv plus at least one clinical isolate, since activity can be strain-dependent. And a single one-week endpoint can miss a bacteriostatic-then-rebound pattern — a time course would catch that a single OD reading won't.",
    "notes": "AI-drafted seed (Claude) — needs Adrian/domain review before use in a large-scale generation run."
  },
  {
    "id": "claude-methcrit-003",
    "submitted_at": "2026-08-06T00:00:00",
    "submitter": "Claude",
    "category": "methodology_critique",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "The user describes a transcriptomics experiment design involving M. tuberculosis. Evaluate the design for statistical and biological validity and flag any issues.",
    "input": "We're planning an RNA-seq experiment comparing Mtb gene expression under normoxia vs hypoxia. We'll grow one culture under each condition, harvest RNA, and sequence. Good to go?",
    "output": "Not quite yet — the main issue is n=1 per condition. With one culture per group you can't separate a true condition effect from ordinary culture-to-culture variation, and differential expression tools like DESeq2 or edgeR need biological replicates (3+ per condition is standard) to even estimate dispersion properly. I'd also confirm your hypoxia model is actually inducing hypoxic adaptation before trusting the comparison — the Wayne model with a defined headspace ratio, plus a marker like DosR regulon induction, is the usual way to validate that. Watch for batch effects too: if replicates across conditions are grown or harvested on different days, randomize/interleave them or include batch as a covariate in the model. Mtb's GC-rich genome and thick cell wall also make RNA extraction and rRNA depletion trickier than for many organisms, so it's worth confirming RNA integrity and depletion efficiency before sequencing. Last thing: hypoxic adaptation is dynamic (NRP1 vs NRP2 stages look different), so a single timepoint may not capture what you're after — a short time course is often more informative than one snapshot.",
    "notes": "AI-drafted seed (Claude) — needs Adrian/domain review before use in a large-scale generation run."
  },
  {
    "id": "claude-extract-001",
    "submitted_at": "2026-08-06T00:00:00",
    "submitter": "Claude",
    "category": "structured_extraction",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a passage from TB literature and a specific extraction request, pull out exactly the requested information in a clean, structured format (e.g. a table or list). Do not add outside information or interpretation beyond what's stated in the passage.",
    "input": "Passage: In this study, the minimum inhibitory concentrations (MICs) of four first-line anti-tuberculosis drugs were determined against the H37Rv reference strain using the microplate Alamar Blue assay. Isoniazid showed an MIC of 0.05 ug/mL, rifampicin an MIC of 0.03 ug/mL, ethambutol an MIC of 2.0 ug/mL, and pyrazinamide an MIC of 25 ug/mL at pH 5.5, consistent with its requirement for acidic conditions to show activity in vitro.\n\nRequest: Extract the drug names and their MIC values into a table.",
    "output": "| Drug | MIC (ug/mL) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Isoniazid | 0.05 | — |\n| Rifampicin | 0.03 | — |\n| Ethambutol | 2.0 | — |\n| Pyrazinamide | 25 | Tested at pH 5.5 (requires acidic conditions for in vitro activity) |",
    "notes": "AI-drafted seed (Claude) — needs Adrian/domain review before use in a large-scale generation run."
  },
  {
    "id": "claude-extract-002",
    "submitted_at": "2026-08-06T00:00:00",
    "submitter": "Claude",
    "category": "structured_extraction",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a passage from TB literature and a specific extraction request, pull out exactly the requested information in a clean, structured format (e.g. a table or list). Do not add outside information or interpretation beyond what's stated in the passage.",
    "input": "Passage: Rifampicin resistance in Mycobacterium tuberculosis is most commonly associated with mutations in the rpoB gene, particularly within an 81-bp region known as the rifampicin resistance-determining region (RRDR). The most frequently observed mutation is S531L, followed by H526Y and D516V. Isoniazid resistance is primarily linked to mutations in katG, especially the S315T substitution, which accounts for the majority of high-level isoniazid-resistant clinical isolates, as well as mutations in the inhA promoter region, which tend to confer low-level resistance.\n\nRequest: Extract each drug, its associated gene, and the specific mutations mentioned, as a list.",
    "output": "- Rifampicin — gene: rpoB (RRDR region). Mutations: S531L (most frequent), H526Y, D516V.\n- Isoniazid — gene: katG. Mutation: S315T (majority of high-level resistance).\n- Isoniazid — gene: inhA (promoter region). Mutations: unspecified promoter mutations (low-level resistance).",
    "notes": "AI-drafted seed (Claude) — needs Adrian/domain review before use in a large-scale generation run."
  },
  {
    "id": "claude-extract-003",
    "submitted_at": "2026-08-06T00:00:00",
    "submitter": "Claude",
    "category": "structured_extraction",
    "ambiguous": false,
    "instruction": "Given a passage from TB literature and a specific extraction request, pull out exactly the requested information in a clean, structured format (e.g. a table or list). Do not add outside information or interpretation beyond what's stated in the passage.",
    "input": "Passage: Mycobacterium tuberculosis H37Rv was cultured in Middlebrook 7H9 broth supplemented with 10% OADC and 0.05% Tween 80 at 37C with shaking at 100 rpm. For hypoxic adaptation, cultures were sealed in Wayne tubes with a headspace ratio of 0.5 and stirred slowly, and samples were harvested at day 0, day 4 (NRP1), and day 10 (NRP2) based on methylene blue decolorization as an indicator of oxygen depletion.\n\nRequest: Extract the strain, media/culture conditions, and sampling timepoints as a structured list.",
    "output": "- Strain: M. tuberculosis H37Rv\n- Media: Middlebrook 7H9 broth + 10% OADC + 0.05% Tween 80\n- Aerobic culture conditions: 37C, shaking at 100 rpm\n- Hypoxia model: Wayne tubes, headspace ratio 0.5, slow stirring\n- Sampling timepoints: Day 0 (baseline), Day 4 (NRP1), Day 10 (NRP2) — staging based on methylene blue decolorization",
    "notes": "AI-drafted seed (Claude) — needs Adrian/domain review before use in a large-scale generation run."
  }
